Yes, your code creates a `Users` row when a person logs in with Google for the first time.

The flow is:

```text
User visits /auth/google
→ Google authenticates the user
→ /auth/google/callback runs
→ findOrCreateUser(profile) runs
→ Users.findOne(...)
→ Users.create(...) if no existing user exists
→ subscription row is created
→ JWT is issued
```

However, I found several issues you should fix.

### 1. Google uses `profile.id`, not `profile.userId`

Your code currently has:

```js
const providerUserId = profile.userId;
```

Change it to:

```js
const providerUserId = profile.id;
```

Otherwise, `providerUserId` will be undefined and user lookup/creation may fail.

### 2. Your username fallback is not unique

You currently have:

```js
const username = profile.username || "GeoGuesser Player";
```

Since `username` is unique in your database, the second user who does not have a Google username will cause a duplicate-key error.

A better temporary approach is:

```js
const email = profile.emails?.[0]?.value;

const username =
  profile.displayName ||
  email?.split("@")[0] ||
  `player_${profile.id}`;
```

But display names can also collide. The safest approach is to generate a unique username:

```js
const baseUsername =
  profile.displayName ||
  email?.split("@")[0] ||
  "player";

const username = `${baseUsername}_${profile.id}`
  .toLowerCase()
  .replace(/[^a-z0-9_]/g, "_")
  .slice(0, 255);
```

You could later let users change this username through a profile-settings route.

### 3. Update `findOrCreateUser`

Use the actual database row consistently instead of returning a second custom user object:

```js
const findOrCreateUser = async (profile) => {
  const providerUserId = profile.id;
  const email = profile.emails?.[0]?.value;

  if (!email) {
    throw new Error("Google profile did not include an email address");
  }

  const existingUser = await Users.findOne({
    where: {
      authProvider: "google",
      providerUserId,
    },
  });

  if (existingUser) {
    return existingUser;
  }

  const username = `${profile.displayName || "player"}_${profile.id}`
    .toLowerCase()
    .replace(/[^a-z0-9_]/g, "_")
    .slice(0, 255);

  const user = await sequelize.transaction(async (transaction) => {
    const newUser = await Users.create(
      {
        email,
        username,
        authProvider: "google",
        providerUserId,
      },
      { transaction }
    );

    await Subscriptions.create(
      {
        userId: newUser.userId,
        status: "pending_payment",
      },
      { transaction }
    );

    return newUser;
  });

  return user;
};
```

Then Passport receives the actual Sequelize user:

```js
async (accessToken, refreshToken, profile, done) => {
  try {
    const user = await findOrCreateUser(profile);
    return done(null, user);
  } catch (error) {
    return done(error, null);
  }
}
```

